  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, where $Y$ takes values $p,$ $q,$ and $r$ with probabilities $0.5,$ $0.3,$ and $0.2,$ respectively. You are given the following conditional expectations:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=p]=8,\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=q]=14,\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=r]=10.\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\text{E}[X]?$",
  "worked_solution": "We are given the conditional expectations of $X$ given different values of another random variable $Y,$ along with the probabilities of those values. This is a direct application of the law of total expectation, which states:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{y}\\text{E}[X∣Y=y]\\cdot P(Y=y).\n$$\n\nIn this problem, $Y$ takes three values:\n\n- $Y=p$ with probability $0.5,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=p]=8$\n\n- $Y=q$ with probability $0.3,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=q]=14$\n\n- $Y=r$ with probability $0.2,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=r]=10$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=p]\\cdot P(Y=p)+\\text{E}[X∣Y=q]\\cdot P(Y=q) \\\\  & \\,+\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=r]\\cdot P(Y=r) \\\\  & =8\\cdot 0.5+14\\cdot 0.3+10\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =4+4.2+2 \\\\  & =10.2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
